WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.649
In a 360-degree film, a cut can throw the subject behind your viewer.

2
00:00:05.669 --> 00:00:09.549
Orbit Cut lets an editor fix that jump from inside the film.

3
00:00:09.569 --> 00:00:12.620
Pinch the orange wheel and turn the next shot into place.

4
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:16.765
Compare Original and Corrected.

5
00:00:16.785 --> 00:00:20.554
The viewing direction stays fixed; the shot rotates.

6
00:00:20.574 --> 00:00:22.924
The viewer can keep following the story.

7
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:31.487
The workbench holds the corrected previous shot on the left and the next shot on the right.

8
00:00:31.507 --> 00:00:34.926
Follow the orange next-step button to the second cut.

9
00:00:34.946 --> 00:00:37.403
Each shot keeps its own correction.

10
00:00:37.423 --> 00:00:39.892
The practice tracks both cuts as you go.

11
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.800
Step away whenever you need to.

12
00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:49.763
Pause keeps the film, the chosen cut and both rotations.

13
00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:52.388
Continue returns to the same work.

14
00:00:52.408 --> 00:00:57.612
Leaving and re-entering immersion also preserves the current session while this tab stays open.

15
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:03.742
Review the full film around you, then export.

16
00:01:03.762 --> 00:01:09.723
Orbit Cut renders the changed video pixels and carries the soundtrack into a local MP4.

17
00:01:09.743 --> 00:01:13.835
Reopening the saved file shows the correction in the video itself.

18
00:01:13.855 --> 00:01:18.596
The file also carries explicit 360-degree projection metadata.

19
00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:26.454
Bring a local two-to-one 360 clip, or try the attributed waterfall exercise.

20
00:01:26.474 --> 00:01:28.775
Your footage stays in the browser.

21
00:01:28.795 --> 00:01:35.264
Built with Meta's Immersive Web SDK, Orbit Cut is a seated editing workbench for storytellers.

22
00:01:35.284 --> 00:01:37.612
Open the preview and try two cuts.

